From 4432d28d1332c9b2d58f305d8361bf7538c50ea4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DisplayXR <dev@displayxr.invalid>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 21:07:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#171): the second edge -- flatten when nothing is
 weavable, and never snapshot a zero-identity rect on an identity page

Patch 0121 held the browser#159 whole-panel FLAT until the new page's first
submit. That closes the navigation that DROPS demand. The storefront's "next"
does not: it is an SPA route change inside one inline-3D session, demand never
moves, no FLAT is ever latched, and the hold has nothing to hold. Measured on
"next", header text ("Account / Sign in") lensed and staying that way until a
scroll or a hover repainted the page:

  210102.490  batch weave: keeping PREVIOUS content for target 0 at 2351,856
  210104.249  NO match for target=2351,856 1100x1100 ... score=289/1000
  210104.328  GPU canvas-layer lookup returned NO resource:
                id=00000000000000000000000000000000 rect=2351,856 1100x1100
  210104.333  withheld 1 of 1 weave rects ...
  210105.551  batch weave: n=1                       <- new tile, ~1.2 s later
  210107.147  GPU canvas-layer lookup returned NO resource:
                id=00000000000000000000000000000000 rect=662,881 1145x1145

Two defects on that transition, fixed here as two halves.

(1) THE ZERO-IDENTITY RECT (display.cc). The `id=0000...` lines are the legacy
main-thread channel: the aggregator admits, by cardinality (0094), the
difference between the layers Blink reports and the layers cc tracks, with no
identity. On an SPA route change that difference is routine -- the outgoing
route's layer is still registered while the incoming canvas has not painted --
and the admitted rect lands wherever getBoundingClientRect put it. It cannot
be marked by browser#167 (no layer id), so it takes the "nothing resolved at
all, snapshot the page" escape, the GPU has no canvas for it, snapshots the 2D
page at that rect, weaves it, and draws it back. That is 2D pixels interlaced
INTO the page, and they stay until the next repaint -- exactly "until I scroll
or hover".

The escape was written for a page with no identity anywhere. On a frame that
carries ANY identity rect, a zero-id rect is now not prepared and is withheld,
counted on the existing LOG(ERROR). A pure-legacy page is untouched.

(2) NOTHING WEAVABLE => FLAT (weave client + the root-pass hook). Once the
outgoing tile stops and the incoming one is withheld, the browser submits
nothing for ~1.2 s and the runtime's sticky wish keeps the OLD rect lensed
over the new page's 2D. The browser cannot retract the wish (a zero-rect batch
is refused), so the GPU side now flattens itself: the root-pass hook reports,
once per root-sized frame, whether the frame had anything weavable; after
kAutoFlatEmptyFrames (3, ~50 ms -- one frame is a web#12 resolve hiccup and
must not flicker the lens; this transition is ~70) the client applies the
whole-panel FLAT on top of the tracker's latest set and records that set as
the held release. The next successful submit releases it, exactly as 0121's
hold does, so the lens comes back the moment the new tile does.

A static page never reaches this: no frames, no empties. Only a page that is
compositing with nothing to weave does, and for that page FLAT is the correct
rendering. A tracker set that itself flattens supersedes the auto flat; a
tracker set that would un-flatten while it is active is held, by the same
rule as 0121.
